Either he's behind,

00:27:39.908 --> 00:27:40.827
he want to jack you,

00:27:41.808 --> 00:27:42.989
or he want this neighborhood.

00:27:43.689 --> 00:27:44.830
Or you want the neighborhood.

00:27:45.570 --> 00:27:47.171
So everybody want to be Scarface.

00:27:48.223 --> 00:27:49.264
Crips killing Crips.

00:27:49.905 --> 00:27:50.925
Crips killing Bloods.

00:27:50.985 --> 00:27:52.026
Bloods killing Bloods.

00:27:52.126 --> 00:27:52.807
Bloods killing

00:27:53.207 --> 00:27:55.288
Crips. The thing just went haywire.

00:27:55.289 --> 00:27:59.091
The drugs came in and had everybody going crazy.

00:28:01.693 --> 00:28:02.493
It's all negative.

00:28:02.994 --> 00:28:03.674
You know what I'm saying?

00:28:03.734 --> 00:28:07.617
But for any man to get in a car to go and drive by and shoot somebody,

00:28:07.937 --> 00:28:08.838
that's a coward's move.

00:28:09.138 --> 00:28:11.639
Because the person that you might be shooting at,

00:28:13.421 --> 00:28:14.361
the bullet might not hit him.

00:28:14.362 --> 00:28:16.603
The bullet might just miss and go through somebody's house.

00:28:16.991 --> 00:28:18.572
Kill a baby in the bed or anything.

00:28:18.892 --> 00:28:24.096
Drive-bys are one of those mythologies of gang life where everybody seems to think,

00:28:24.196 --> 00:28:24.537
again,

00:28:24.617 --> 00:28:25.938
I'm afraid fed by the media,

00:28:26.618 --> 00:28:28.459
that most gang homicides are drive-bys.

00:28:28.780 --> 00:28:29.300
They're not.

00:28:30.641 --> 00:28:33.483
That drive-bys are almost always fatal.

00:28:33.783 --> 00:28:34.324
They are not.

00:28:34.424 --> 00:28:36.746
Most of them do not yield death.

00:28:37.386 --> 00:28:39.888
While no drive-by statistics were collected in the early 80s,

00:28:39.889 --> 00:28:43.891
by 1991 they were occurring at an average rate of four per day.

00:28:44.703 --> 00:28:46.925
Two thousand people were shot in those attacks,

00:28:47.025 --> 00:28:48.066
with less than 10%

00:28:48.086 --> 00:28:49.447
of those shots proving fatal.

00:28:51.489 --> 00:28:54.011
Despite the media's focus on drive-by shootings,

00:28:54.471 --> 00:29:00.937
the fact remains that most of the nearly 800 gang-related homicides charted that year occurred in some other fashion.

00:29:01.117 --> 00:29:05.280
The symbolic nature of a drive-by is what captures our attention.

00:29:05.641 --> 00:29:06.101
Throughout the

00:29:06.862 --> 00:29:11.045
1980s, images and symbols characteristic of modern gang culture solidified.

00:29:11.506 --> 00:29:12.907
All gangs have hand signs.

00:29:13.367 --> 00:29:15.569
By the time you get to know what one of them's hand sign is,

00:29:15.609 --> 00:29:16.730
it changes to something else.

00:29:16.850 --> 00:29:20.073
The very first gang sign was done by the Slausons,

00:29:20.833 --> 00:29:23.576
and they used to throw up a V for Slauson Village.

00:29:24.116 --> 00:29:25.257
And after they threw up the V,

00:29:25.637 --> 00:29:28.340
all the other clubs adapted their own hand signs.

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:29.641
Real general,

00:29:29.681 --> 00:29:30.522
if you throw up a C,

00:29:31.122 --> 00:29:31.863
that means crip,

00:29:32.623 --> 00:29:33.624
and if you throw up a B,

00:29:33.964 --> 00:29:35.506
a variety of ways you can throw up a B,

00:29:36.106 --> 00:29:36.927
which means blood.

00:29:37.427 --> 00:29:38.849
Gang members will throw signs,

00:29:38.929 --> 00:29:39.971
make a hand sign,

00:29:40.051 --> 00:29:40.792
add another gang,

00:29:41.352 --> 00:29:43.275
and then he'll answer back with his,

00:29:43.896 --> 00:29:44.537
or gunfire.

00:29:46.139 --> 00:29:48.442
Tattoos generally depict the gang,

00:29:49.003 --> 00:29:50.264
the person's nickname maybe,

00:29:50.725 --> 00:29:52.548
and his philosophical bent,

00:29:52.608 --> 00:29:53.869
whether he be a crip or blood.

00:29:54.450 --> 00:29:55.071
A crip would have...

00:29:55.151 --> 00:29:57.273
There may be BK Blood Killer tattooed on him.

00:29:57.813 --> 00:29:59.235
A blood would have CK

00:29:59.575 --> 00:30:00.976
Crip Killer tattooed on him.

00:30:01.216 --> 00:30:02.297
Does that mean you're a Crip Killer?

00:30:03.618 --> 00:30:05.080
You ever put any work in for the gang?

00:30:05.081 --> 00:30:06.421
You shoot anybody that you're a cop to?

00:30:06.821 --> 00:30:08.282
I'm not violent.

00:30:08.283 --> 00:30:13.747
I'm sure all the kids get to play at this little park right here on the colorful slide with all the markings.

00:30:14.207 --> 00:30:17.090
There's a variety of different writing conventions going on in graffiti.

00:30:17.370 --> 00:30:18.371
First and foremost,

00:30:18.431 --> 00:30:19.352
the name of the gang.

00:30:20.093 --> 00:30:22.735
It's about advertising your gang.

00:30:23.303 --> 00:30:25.865
And then there are symbolic things going on,

00:30:25.925 --> 00:30:26.886
such as arrows,

00:30:26.946 --> 00:30:29.769
which mean that this neighborhood is being claimed by that turf,

00:30:29.789 --> 00:30:31.490
and you'll see arrows pointing down.

00:30:31.891 --> 00:30:34.153
There's also representation of the dead,

00:30:34.493 --> 00:30:37.736
sort of commemorating gang members who've died in conflict.

00:30:38.236 --> 00:30:42.500
Tombstone-like symbols with the names of members written inside them.

00:30:42.981 --> 00:30:43.461
And in fact,

00:30:43.481 --> 00:30:46.083
sometimes they'll even tattoo those same things on their bodies.

00:30:46.364 --> 00:30:50.047
Alonzo found that graffiti was a good indication of a gang's territory.

00:30:50.367 --> 00:30:56.370
as warring gangs tended to deface the graffiti of their rivals with greatest frequency at the boundaries of their influence.

00:30:56.911 --> 00:31:01.833
This wall marks the line between the turf of the Roland 30s Crips and the Roland 20s Bloods.

00:31:02.414 --> 00:31:04.795
Crip gangs will often X out the letters B and P,

00:31:05.275 --> 00:31:07.657
representative of the Piru or Bloodsets.

00:31:08.577 --> 00:31:11.319
This is true even when they're writing the P in their own name.

00:31:12.179 --> 00:31:13.840
Bloodsets respond in kind,

00:31:13.960 --> 00:31:14.961
avoiding the letter C.

00:31:16.361 --> 00:31:19.023
Every name possesses an inflammatory counterpart.

00:31:19.735 --> 00:31:23.997
The chicken in this example is a derogatory representation of the kitchen crips,

00:31:24.497 --> 00:31:28.959
while the crying toast is an antagonistic pun aimed at the East Coast crips.

00:31:29.239 --> 00:31:29.439
Well,

00:31:29.479 --> 00:31:33.941
one of the old sayings of it was that it was the newspaper to the streets.

00:31:34.501 --> 00:31:37.022
We look and see if two gangs haven't been marking each other out,

00:31:37.062 --> 00:31:38.543
and all of a sudden they're marking each other out.

00:31:39.103 --> 00:31:39.804
We don't know why,

00:31:39.904 --> 00:31:41.484
and there's a problem that has developed,

00:31:41.544 --> 00:31:43.985
because that's a capital offense within the gang world.

00:31:44.246 --> 00:31:47.407
And I can't tell you how many we've found dead at the scene of something like that.

00:31:47.703 --> 00:31:51.245
The fights and the disputes and the conflicts are never really over the turf.

00:31:52.125 --> 00:31:56.427
It's just over the identity of the other organization.

00:32:01.329 --> 00:32:03.270
Gang identity is a powerful force,

00:32:03.450 --> 00:32:07.391
and loyalty is tested in ways designed to separate the homies from the wannabes.

00:32:07.972 --> 00:32:11.033
This process has given rise to a unique turn of phrase.

00:32:11.333 --> 00:32:12.634
If I tell you to put in work,

00:32:13.314 --> 00:32:14.975
you should know already what I'm talking about.

00:32:14.995 --> 00:32:15.695
That means...

00:32:16.639 --> 00:32:17.860
You're putting in work for the hood.

00:32:17.920 --> 00:32:22.742
That means get your pistol and go down there and put in work.

00:32:23.023 --> 00:32:27.025
That's how gang members become celebrities within the community,

00:32:27.125 --> 00:32:28.786
by putting in a certain amount of work.

00:32:29.766 --> 00:32:31.187
And if it results in a homicide,

00:32:31.227 --> 00:32:32.788
then you really put in work.

00:32:35.589 --> 00:32:38.911
The issue of gang cohesiveness is central to my way of thinking about gangs.

00:32:39.411 --> 00:32:40.872
Once gangs have formed,

00:32:41.533 --> 00:32:42.133
the question is,

00:32:42.553 --> 00:32:43.654
why do they maintain themselves?

00:32:43.674 --> 00:32:44.534
Why do they keep going?

00:32:44.535 --> 00:32:45.795
And there are several reasons for that.

00:32:46.299 --> 00:32:48.340
One is that their activities hold them together.

00:32:48.520 --> 00:32:50.080
That's why they joined in the first place,

00:32:50.460 --> 00:32:52.941
and they're held together by their own very nature.

00:32:53.421 --> 00:32:53.881
But also,

00:32:54.841 --> 00:32:56.402
people start responding to gangs.

00:32:56.582 --> 00:32:59.963
The neighborhood starts turning its back on those youngsters,

00:33:00.303 --> 00:33:02.063
and the youngsters feel more alienated.

00:33:02.524 --> 00:33:04.304
Police crackdowns may take place,

00:33:04.724 --> 00:33:06.845
giving the gang something to fight against,

00:33:07.565 --> 00:33:08.665
a kind of common enemy,

00:33:08.805 --> 00:33:09.225
if you will.

00:33:09.566 --> 00:33:11.306
So that once the gang is formed,

00:33:11.786 --> 00:33:14.727
then social responses to the gang kick in.

00:33:15.431 --> 00:33:20.235
And reinforce the very alienation that brought those youngsters into the gang in the first place,

00:33:20.695 --> 00:33:22.336
reinforcing their bonds to the group,

00:33:22.456 --> 00:33:24.097
reinforcing gang cohesiveness.

00:33:24.478 --> 00:33:26.139
That's a very hard thing to avoid.

00:33:26.899 --> 00:33:29.822
What I would do if I were a police organization,

00:33:29.842 --> 00:33:30.562
I had my drugs,

00:33:30.582 --> 00:33:34.025
would be to put a great deal more money into community development,

00:33:34.085 --> 00:33:36.066
into prevention efforts early on,

00:33:36.506 --> 00:33:38.868
rather than trying to handle the problem of the 16,

00:33:38.928 --> 00:33:39.649
17,

00:33:39.669 --> 00:33:42.531
18-year-old gang member who is already fully committed to that group.

00:33:42.931 --> 00:33:45.652
and not likely to be affected by your suppressive efforts.

00:33:46.272 --> 00:33:48.112
If you put him away for three or four or five years,

00:33:48.373 --> 00:33:49.253
he's going to come back.

00:33:49.593 --> 00:33:51.093
He's going to come back more hardened,

00:33:51.653 --> 00:33:53.954
more convinced that nobody loves him,

00:33:54.294 --> 00:33:55.494
and he's going to respond to that.

00:34:00.216 --> 00:34:03.937
Both the news and entertainment media function as agents of gang cohesion.

00:34:04.557 --> 00:34:05.917
By focusing on violence,

00:34:06.057 --> 00:34:10.198
it could be argued that the media reward gangs for their sensational behavior.

00:34:10.759 --> 00:34:11.919
There was an attraction.

00:34:12.575 --> 00:34:14.677
Particularly in Hollywood and the TV industry,

00:34:15.197 --> 00:34:21.902
of showing gang members and making them bad guys,

00:34:22.042 --> 00:34:24.744
but the old Clint Eastwood type of guy,

00:34:26.665 --> 00:34:27.906
the anti-hero type.

00:34:28.326 --> 00:34:31.048
And that's not them at all.

00:34:31.248 --> 00:34:31.829
They're thugs.

00:34:32.189 --> 00:34:34.110
We spread the word about gangs through TV,

00:34:34.290 --> 00:34:34.971
through MTV,

00:34:35.131 --> 00:34:36.292
through the print media,

00:34:36.372 --> 00:34:37.412
through movies and so on.

00:34:38.233 --> 00:34:39.414
There's not a kid in America,

00:34:39.474 --> 00:34:40.815
probably an adolescent at least.

00:34:41.091 --> 00:34:43.493
Who doesn't know what a gang member presumably looks like,

00:34:43.553 --> 00:34:44.093
talks like,

00:34:44.213 --> 00:34:44.774
